'''Yamagata-ken Gokoku Jinja''' (山形県護国神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|Shinto shrine|WD=Q845945|lt=Shinto shrine|1=Shinto shrine}} ({{ill|Gokoku shrines|en|Gokoku shrines|WD=Q1534477|lt=Gokoku Shrine|1=Gokoku Shrines}}) located in {{ill|Yamagata (city)|en|Yamagata (city)|WD=Q205526|lt=Yamagata (city)|1=Yamagata (city)}}, {{ill|Yamagata Prefecture|en|Yamagata Prefecture|WD=Q125863|lt=Yamagata Prefecture|1=Yamagata Prefecture}}.  {{ill|Japan|en|Japan|WD=Q17|lt=Japan|1=Japan}}. It is a [[Beppyo Shrine]], or a shrine that is particularly notable in a certain way with a significant history to it. It was founded in 1868, and is located in {{ill|Yamagata Prefecture|en|Yamagata Prefecture|WD=Q125863|lt=Yamagata Prefecture|1=Yamagata Prefecture}}.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yassan |date=2021-11-29 |title=Tiger in Yamagata prefecture Gokoku shrine, Yamagata City 25km |url=https://gpsart.info/en/tohoku-2/tiger-in-yamagata-prefecture-gokoku-shrine-yamagata-city-25km/ |access-date=2023-11-27 |website=GPS Art .info |language=en-GB}}</ref> It is a [[Gokoku Shrines|Gokoku Shrine]], or a shrine dedicated to war dead. Such shrines were made to serve to enshrine the war dead, and they were all considered "branches" of {{ill|Yasukuni Shrine|en|Yasukuni Shrine|WD=Q242803|lt=Yasukuni Shrine|1=Yasukuni Shrine}}. They were renamed from {{ill|Shōkonsha|en|Shōkonsha|WD=Q11497810|lt=Shokonsha|1=Shōkonsha}} in 1939.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=TAKAYAMA |first=K. PETER |date=1990 |title=Enshrinement and Persistency of Japanese Religion |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23917081 |journal=Journal of Church and State |volume=32 |issue=3 |pages=527–547 |doi=10.1093/jcs/32.3.527 |jstor=23917081 |issn=0021-969X}}</ref> It enshrines over 48,000 spirits of those from Yamagata Prefecture who died in wars from the {{ill|Meiji Restoration|en|Meiji Restoration|WD=Q8707|lt=Meiji Restoration|1=Meiji Restoration}} through the {{ill|Second Sino-Japanese War|en|Second Sino-Japanese War|WD=Q170314|lt=Second Sino-Japanese War|1=Second Sino-Japanese War}} and {{ill|World War II|en|World War II|WD=Q362|lt=World War II|1=World War II}}.
[[File:Yamagata Pref. Gokoku-jinja longshot.jpg|thumb|250px|Ōtorii (and haiden in the background)]]

== History ==
The shrine traces its origins to Meiji 2 (1869), when a hall was constructed in Yōka-chō, Yamagata City, to enshrine the spirits of ten {{ill|Satsuma Domain|en|Satsuma Domain|WD=Q676187|lt=Satsuma Domain|1=Satsuma Domain}} soldiers who died in the {{ill|Boshin War|en|Boshin War|WD=Q309184|lt=Boshin War|1=Boshin War}}. In Meiji 8, it was designated as Yamagata {{ill|Shōkonsha|ja|招魂社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shōkonsha|comment=changed title based on wikidata|1=Shōkonsha|WD=Q11497810}}, and subsequently enshrined war dead from Yamagata Prefecture. In Meiji 22 (1889), it was relocated to Miyamachi, Yamagata City, but was destroyed by fire in a large blaze in 1911 (Meiji 44), and rebuilt in 1914 (Taishō 3). In Shōwa 9 (1934), it moved to its current location inside Chitose Park. In Shōwa 14 (1939), with institutional reforms, it was designated as an Interior Minister-approved Gokoku Jinja and named Yamagata-ken Gokoku Jinja. Under the Allied Occupation, from Shōwa 22 (1947) to Shōwa 27 (1952), it was called “Chitose-gū,” referencing Chitose Park. The current haiden was rebuilt in Heisei 6 (1994).

== Hatsumōde (First Shrine Visit of the New Year) ==

Yamagata-ken Gokoku Jinja attracts the largest number of hatsumōde (New Year’s first visits) worshippers in Yamagata Prefecture, with about 130,000 visitors annually.<ref>[http://www.yamagataken-gokokujinja.jp/history.shtml Official site: History (in Japanese)]</ref><ref>[http://yamagata-np.jp/news/200901/05/kj_2009010500051.php “91,6000 People Visit Shrines in the Prefecture for New Year: Up by 33,000 from Last Year”] Yamagata Shimbun (January 5, 2009), accessed September 7, 2011.</ref><ref>[http://yamagata-np.jp/news/201101/04/kj_2011010400048.php “Over 1 Million Visit Shrines for Hatsumōde in Prefecture, Highest in a Decade”] Yamagata Shimbun (January 4, 2011), accessed September 7, 2011.</ref> The second most visited shrine is {{ill|Chōkai Gassan Ryōsho-gū|ja|鳥海月山両所宮|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Chōkai Gassan Ryōsho-gū|qq=}} in Yamagata City (~110,000 visitors). Only these two shrines surpass 100,000 visitors in the prefecture.
== New years festival ==
The shrine has the largest {{ill|Hatsumōde|en|Hatsumōde|WD=Q1032381|lt=Hatsumōde|1=Hatsumōde}} festival of {{ill|Yamagata Prefecture|en|Yamagata Prefecture|WD=Q125863|lt=Yamagata Prefecture|1=Yamagata Prefecture}} with 130,000 people attending. Second most is {{ill|Chōkai Gassan Ryōsho-gū|simple|Chōkai Gassan Ryōsho-gū|ja|鳥海月山両所宮|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Chōkai gassan ryōsho-gu|qq=}}
